<p>We&#8217;re striking up the band with some musically inclined macOS and iOS apps this week. In some cases, these apps augment music-subscription services like Apple Music, but the main point is to let you enjoy the music stored on your Apple device in new and different ways.</p>
<p>If you&#8217;ve got an app that marches to the beat of its own drummer, let us hear about it. Email <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code> with a description of the macOS or iOS offering you want to bring to our attention. We won&#8217;t be able to write back due to the volume of submissions we get, but we&#8217;ll certainly give your app a look for inclusion in a future column.</p>
<h2>Needl</h2>
<figure><img height="1841" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/needl-combo-6c-1.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Three iPhone screenshots of a vinyl record collection app: a library with Your Shelves and Your Collection (evermore, Cats), an AI chat about Taylor Swift with Try Asking prompts, and an Add to Library screen for Olivia Rodrigo's GUTS." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Many people still collect vinyl records, though I can&#8217;t claim to be one of them. My daughter, however, is known to pick up the occasional album at record shops and flea markets, and an app like <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/needl-vinyl-record-collector/id6754252048">Needl</a> could help her get a handle on her growing collection.</p>
<p>Using Needl on your iPhone, you can scan the barcode on the album cover to import information about it onto your phone. (A Cover Scan feature identifies albums by the sleeve artwork, but you&#8217;ll need the Pro version for that feature.) Once imported, you can choose the edition/pressing of the album; Needl also lets you log the CDs and cassettes you have on hand.</p>
<p>Besides providing a handy glance at your record collection, Needl lets you keep a wishlist, shows the value of records if you&#8217;re looking to sell or buy and even offers AI-powered answers to questions about the artist or album. (One complaint: in the version I tested, a suggested Try Asking box remained on the screen, making it hard to see the complete answer.)</p>
<p>There&#8217;s a basic visualizer feature in the free download of Needl, but upgrading to the $20/year Pro version adds a custom visualizer and live lyrics feature to the app. (Weekly and monthly subscriptions are available, too.)</p>
<p>Perhaps the greatest value of Needl comes from its ability to connect to music services like Apple Music, Spotify and Tidal, as you can thumb through your collection and decide what kind of song or record to play next.</p>
<h2>Albumary</h2>
<figure><img height="1652" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary1-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of the Albumary Mac app on a dark record-store background. A Kaiser Chiefs album, &quot;Yours Truly, Angry Mob,&quot; is enlarged in the center, with a &quot;tap for the songs&quot; link. Kajagoogoo and Limahl's &quot;Too Shy&quot; album is visible at left." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/albumary/id6797989082">Albumary</a> brings a different look to music management for the songs and albums stored on your Mac. Instead of the database structure found in the built-in Music app, Albumary adopts the look of a real-world music store, as you thumb through your collection like you would through a store rack full of records.</p>
<p>You can organize albums and singles by genre, decade or alphabetically, and flip through them by clicking on a stack of records pulled out of the virtual shelves. Flipping over a cover shows you a list of songs, the genre tags and album credits, including — in some cases — the personnel playing on the record.</p>
<figure><img height="1652" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/albumary2-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of the Albumary Mac app showing Kaiser Chiefs' album Yours Truly, Angry Mob: Rock, released 2006, tags indie rock, britpop, a Last.fm summary, track 1 Ruby (3:23), and credits. Parliament's Mothership Connection cover sits at left." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>In an especially nice touch, playlists from your music library are organized as cassettes in the Albumary interface. Anyone who assembled their own mix tapes in the &#8217;80s and &#8217;90s will appreciate that approach.</p>
<p>You can use Albumary for free for 21 days. After that, you&#8217;ll need to pay $18 to keep your virtual record store intact on your Mac.</p>
<h2>Truffle</h2>
<figure><img height="1520" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2360" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/truffle-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of a Mac music library cleanup app showing an Apple Music track list with match percentages. The selected track, &quot;Perhaps, Perhaps, Perhaps&quot; by Desi Arnaz and His Orchestra, is matched, with a sidebar offering to swap in the Deezer album cover." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>If you&#8217;ve spent a long time assembling a music library, you&#8217;ve likely got a lot of entries missing things like cover art, album info and other bits of data. <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/truffle-music-tag-editor/id6806763735?mt=12">Truffle</a> is a macOS app for the completist in all of us, as it will analyze the songs you own looking to fill in any missing details.</p>
<p>Truffle does this by checking for sound rather than file name. Even more important, the app gives you control over imposing any changes, as you&#8217;re given the chance to approve the cover, artist, album year and genre data retrieved by the app. Owing to the developer&#8217;s roots as a hobbyist DJ, Truffle also works with Traktor, Serato and Rekordbox collections.</p>
<p>You can try Truffle for free, with analysis of up to 100 tracks. Unlocking the full app costs $50.</p>
<h2>Katalog</h2>
<figure><img height="1725" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/katalog-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Screenshot of Katalog's Prep view playing &quot;The Only Living Boy In New York&quot; by Simon &amp; Garfunkel (key B, 76 BPM). A track list sits at center, a Matches sidebar lists tracks pairing with it, and the library sidebar is on the left." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Like Truffle, <a href="https://musickatalog.com">Katalog</a> is built for people who are serious about collecting music, having amassed a lot of tracks over the years. Katalog&#8217;s developer says the app can open a 91,000-track library in less than a second, so the 2,500 or so tracks I have stored on my Mac are no match for it.</p>
<p>Katalog looks a lot like what would have happened if Apple had kept the old iTunes interface in place and then just kept adding to it. It sorts your library by the usual information — artist, title, album, genre and so forth — while adding other categories for serious music fans and DJs like BPM and Key. A number of categories in the side menu like acapella, instrumentals, remixes, masters and more let you further sort your tunes out by type.</p>
<p>Katalog boasts four different modes for viewing your library, including a few aimed at people who make their own music and store it on their Mac. I particularly like the Prep interface aimed at building playlists with songs that have complementary keys and beats.</p>
<p>For basic music playback, Katalog is going to come across as overkill. Instead, this app is for music creators, DJs or people with extensive collections that include rare and offbeat tracks. Basic features in Katalog are free, and if you buy a support license for $30 to unlock extra features during the app&#8217;s beta process, those features are yours for life. Otherwise, the extras will cost $30/year once Katalog 1.0 ships. You can try everything out for 30 days right now.</p>
<h2>GroovSide</h2>
<figure><img height="1795" sizes="(max-width: 1010px) calc(100vw - 60px), 690px" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=690&amp;quality=90 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1035&amp;quality=90 1035w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=1380&amp;quality=90 1380w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2070&amp;quality=90 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&amp;w=2760&amp;quality=90 2760w" width="2760" data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/groovslide2-6c.png?ssl=1&#038;w=1380&#038;quality=90" alt="Illustrated turntable showing a Now Playing display: &quot;Joltin' Joe DiMaggio,&quot; Les Browne Orchestra, Betty Bonney, Baseball: A Film By Ken Burns, 0:31 / 2:52, Side C, 15/31, LP 2/3, Played Today 7. A white record spins beneath a tonearm." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>We started out with vinyl, and that&#8217;s what we&#8217;ll return to at the end of this column — at least vinyl in the virtual sense. <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/groovside/id6826888565?mt=12">GroovSide</a> offers a new look for enjoying music on your Mac by replacing your desktop with an animated turntable that spins as you play songs from either Apple Music or Spotify.</p>
<p>GroovSide is serious about the turntable motif. If you&#8217;re playing a full album, the animation will show the record flipping over when you reach the second side of the playlist. Single songs appear as 7- or 12-inch records. And the album art appears in the center of the record with title, artist and playback info also showing up on the screen.</p>
<p>It might take some fiddling to get GroovSide to work properly. I wasn&#8217;t seeing the animations when I first tested the app, but after emailing with the developer, I learned that I had to go into GroovSide&#8217;s settings to turn off a Pause When Covered toggle — a setting that I&#8217;m guessing was affecting the app since I was testing on a MacBook Air and GroovSide was looking to help preserve battery life. After making that adjustment, though, GroovSide worked as advertised.</p>
<p>No one&#8217;s going to pretend that GroovSide performs a vital function. It&#8217;s all about the whimsy, giving you a more interesting way to control music playback. For some listeners, that&#8217;s more than enough to justify the $5 cost of GroovSide.</p>
